\textbf{The limit $K\to\infty$.} Take logarithms and use the Taylor series $\log(1-x)=-x-\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\tfrac{x^3}{3}-\cdots$, valid for $|x|<1$ (Chapter~\ref{ch:series}), with $x=s/K$:
\begin{align}
\log Q_K(s)&=(K-1)\log\left(1-\frac sK\right)\nonumber\\
&=(K-1)\left(-\frac sK-\frac{s^2}{2K^2}-\cdots\right)\nonumber\\
&=-s+\frac sK-\frac{(K-1)s^2}{2K^2}-\cdots .
\end{align}
Each term after $-s$ carries at least one factor $1/K$. There are infinitely many of them, so we bound their sum rather than treating them one at a time. For $0\le x\le\tfrac12$,
\begin{equation}
\left|\log(1-x)+x\right|=\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{x^3}{3}+\cdots\le\frac{x^2}{2}\left(1+x+x^2+\cdots\right)=\frac{x^2}{2(1-x)}\le x^2 .
\end{equation}
The first inequality replaces each coefficient $1/j$ ($j\ge3$) by the larger $1/2$. The next step sums the geometric series (Chapter~\ref{ch:series}), and the last uses $1-x\ge\tfrac12$. Now write $\log Q_K(s)=(K-1)\left[\log(1-x)+x\right]-(K-1)x$ and note that $(K-1)x=s-s/K$. Then, for $K\ge2s$ (so that $x\le\tfrac12$),
