Let the matrix depend on a control parameter $\tau$ (think of a field strength you are turning up; we avoid $\lambda$, which denotes the eigenvalues), with a fixed coupling $g$:
\begin{equation}
H(\tau)=\begin{pmatrix}\tau & g\\ g & -\tau\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
By Eq.~\eqref{eq:level-statistics-2x2eig} with $a=\tau$, $d=-\tau$, $b=g$, the levels are $\pm\sqrt{\tau^2+g^2}$. Without coupling ($g=0$) the levels $\pm\tau$ cross at $\tau=0$. With any nonzero coupling they never meet: the smallest separation is $2|g|$, reached at $\tau=0$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:level-statistics-avoided}).

This is an identity, valid for any stationary sequence. Solved for $C_1$ it reads
\begin{equation}
C_1=C_0-\tfrac12\mathrm{Var}(\Delta u),
\label{eq:level-statistics-C1}
\end{equation}
so the lag-1 covariance is negative exactly when the fluctuation variance $C_0$ is smaller than half the spacing variance. For the first thousand zeros, computing both sides with qmnt (unfold as in the listing of Sec.~\ref{sec:level-statistics-unfolding}), this is what happens: $C_0<\tfrac12\mathrm{Var}(\Delta u)$, so $C_1<0$, and the spacings vary more than $2C_0$ alone would give. Nothing forces this in general.

\begin{beyond}
For large GUE matrices the variance of the fluctuations $\delta_n$ grows slowly (logarithmically) with the matrix size, while the variance of the unfolded spacings stays near $0.178$. By Eq.~\eqref{eq:level-statistics-C1}, $C_1$ then becomes positive. We quote this growth without derivation; see \cite{mehta}.
\end{beyond}

So the negative lag-1 correlation is a property of the zeros at the heights studied here, not a universal consequence of repulsion.

\textbf{Consecutive spacings.} The alternation of long and short spacings noticed in Table~\ref{tab:level-statistics-re1} is measured by the covariance of consecutive spacings. The mean spacing is one, so $\mathrm{Cov}(\Delta u_n,\Delta u_{n+1})=\langle(\Delta u_n-1)(\Delta u_{n+1}-1)\rangle$, and $\Delta u_n-1=\delta_{n+1}-\delta_n$. Hence
\begin{align}
\mathrm{Cov}(\Delta u_n,\Delta u_{n+1})&=\left\langle(\delta_{n+1}-\delta_n)(\delta_{n+2}-\delta_{n+1})\right\rangle\nonumber\\
&=\langle\delta_{n+1}\delta_{n+2}\rangle-\langle\delta_{n+1}^2\rangle-\langle\delta_n\delta_{n+2}\rangle+\langle\delta_n\delta_{n+1}\rangle\nonumber\\
&=C_1-C_0-C_2+C_1\quad\text{(stationarity)}\nonumber\\
&=-C_0+2C_1-C_2 .
\label{eq:level-statistics-spacingcov}
\end{align}
The term $-C_0$ is always negative, and $2C_1$ is negative whenever $C_1<0$. For the first thousand zeros, computed with qmnt, the correlation of consecutive unfolded spacings is negative: a long spacing tends to be followed by a short one. The sample value of the left side of Eq.~\eqref{eq:level-statistics-spacingcov} agrees with $-C_0+2C_1-C_2$ computed from the same zeros to better than $0.01$. This is the spacing counterpart of the negative fluctuation autocorrelation that the paper reports at lags 1--3.
